  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper-level ridging will persist over the next couple days \nallowing for the continuing trend of warm and dry conditions\nacross the region through Friday. Starting Friday afternoon, the \nridge starts to break down and southwesterly flow aloft starts to \nincrease as an upper-level trough approaches. Surface-level winds\nwill increase through the peak mixing hours combining with warm\ntemperatures and low relative humidity to produce elevated to\ncritical fire weather conditions across much of Central and\nSouthwest Montana through the evening hours.\n\nFriday night, the upper level low begins to move south over the \nOlympic peninsula, cutting off from a dominant band of high-\nlatitude westerlies. This will transition the upper level flow over \nMontana from being relatively zonal to southwesterly, as the low \nforms a positively tilted trough elongating from northern California \nthrough northeastern Washington this weekend. Friday night into \nSaturday morning, a cold front and associated low pressure will \nbuild and move south from the Canadian Rockies, eventually settling \nbroadly over the Great Basin and Northern Great Plains, as high \npressure moves back in towards North-Central MT. This high pressure \nto the north will create northerly flow, which combined with the \ncold front will usher in a cooler airmass through the weekend. This \ncooler air will allow for high temperatures to drop into the 30s/low \n40s Sunday, with the mountains only seeing highs in the 20s/low 30s. \nThe combination of broad WSW flow and the airmass regime sets the \nstage for what may be the first significant, widespread-impactful \nsnowfall of the season late Saturday through early next week.\n- Dzomba \n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nSee FIRE WEATHER for details on the upcoming fire weather concerns\nfor Friday.\n\nWinter Weather This Weekend: The colder, drier air feeding in \nfrom the northern high pressure gives increased confidence of low-\nelevation snow, especially between the Rocky Mountain Front and \nthe Highwoods towards the Canadian border. There is also moderate \nconfidence in the snowfall totals driven by the initial cold \nfrontal surge. However, the combination of the wide baroclinic \nzone with uncertain upper level details, plus local enhancement \nfrom upsloping, leave ample opportunity for locally higher/lower \namounts, and result in broad high/low end probabilities for the \nlate Saturday through Sunday period. In addition, the upper level \nlow may get cut off completely instead of elongating, which gives \nthe forecast overall low confidence. -Dzomba",
